<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ===========================================================================
  THE ALL BRANDS PAGE'S OWN STYLESHEET
  ===========================================================================
  Two components, one request, and nothing this page cannot show.

  css/spartrak-brands.less compiles components/_brand-grid.less (the grid,
  this page only) and components/_cascade-finder.less (the "بتدور علي ايه؟"
  finder, shared with the homepage). That is the whole page.

  WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: load css/spartrak-home.css. That bundle is
  ~3,000 lines of banner, category-tile reveal stage, video showcase, brand
  rail and split promo, and this route renders none of them. Reaching for it
  because it happens to contain the finder's rules would put a page's worth of
  dead CSS on a render-blocking request — which is why the finder was
  extracted into its own partial instead. Same reasoning, and the same
  measurement, as the split recorded in Magento_Cms/layout/cms_index_index.xml.

  THE HANDLE. `spartrak_brand_index_index` is the route's own handle, the same
  one Spartrak_Brand mounts its blocks on
  (app/code/Spartrak/Brand/view/frontend/layout/spartrak_brand_index_index.xml).
  Markup and the CSS that styles it are declared on one handle and cannot
  drift apart.

  WHY THE <css> IS HERE AND THE BLOCKS ARE IN THE MODULE. The module owns page
  STRUCTURE — what renders and in what order is application behaviour, and
  both Spartrak themes inherit it. The theme owns page PRESENTATION. Same
  split as Spartrak_InstaPay's transfer page.

  This file adds nothing else.
-->
<page xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <head>
        <css src="css/spartrak-brands.css"/>
    </head>
</page>
